  "query_preview": "..High pressure is building over Arizona today and\nwill help to diminish Monsoon activity as subsidence increases\nunder the ridge and drier air gradually works its way into the \nregion. Observations from this morning still show dewpoints in the\nlow to mid 50s across the higher terrain, especially from \nFlagstaff southward to the White Mountains. This lingering \nmoisture may still support isolated thunderstorms over the higher \nterrain this afternoon. Storm motion is expected to be east- \nnortheast around 5 mph, while outflow from thunderstorms may \nintroduced a slight southward drift. The slow storm motion will \nwill keep a low-end flash flood threat in place, especially over \nburns scars if thunderstorms develop overhead. This somewhat \nsuppressed period of monsoonal activity is forecast to continue \nthrough at least the weekend. Showers and thunderstorms will not \nfully shut off each day, but remain isolated with the best \ncoverage in the White Mountains.\n\nThe primary hazard over the next few days will be heat. With the \ndecrease in moisture and above normal heights at 500mb,\ntemperatures will be the warmest of the year so far for all of \nnorthern AZ. As a result, a variety of heat headlines will be in \neffect. An Extreme Heat Warning for the Grand Canyon below 4000 ft\nwill remain in effect until Sunday with daytime temperatures up \nto 110-117 F. Additional Extreme Heat Warnings will be in effect \ntoday through Sunday for Glen/Marble canyon and low elevation \nareas south of the Mogollon Rim, where temperatures will approach \nor exceed 110 F. Lower elevations of northeastern Arizona will \nhave a Heat Advisory on Saturday and Sunday, with daytime \ntemperatures up to 100-105 F.\n\nBy the early to middle part of next week, ensemble guidance is \nleaning towards the high weakening and shifting east. This would \nallow monsoonal activity to increase again. There is still some \nuncertainty on the exact timing and nature of this transition, and\nthere is potential that heat headlines will be extended past \nSunday. Right now the forecast is leaning toward hot temperatures\nwith diminished monsoonal activity continuing into Monday and\npotentially Tuesday, before Monsoon moisture starts to make a more\nnoticeable return around Wednesday.",
